WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.473 --> 00:00:01.890
JENNY BROWN: Are
you stuck looking

00:00:01.890 --> 00:00:04.710
for a sweet spot between
user connection speed,

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:07.320
cloud hosting performance,
and the buffer window

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:09.150
you use request data?

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:10.290
Well, you're in luck.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.390
Because today on
"Cloud Storage bytes,"

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:15.190
we're exploring the Venn
diagram that is performance,

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:17.160
and we're going to
find that overlap.

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:17.880
Stay tuned.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.600 --> 00:00:24.370
If you're a company
that streams data,

00:00:24.370 --> 00:00:26.440
performance for
you and your users

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:29.865
is going to be a
reasonably huge priority.

00:00:29.865 --> 00:00:31.240
Let's say you're
a company that's

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:32.890
looking to duplicate
your content

00:00:32.890 --> 00:00:34.930
for an international audience.

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:37.300
Your architecture
setup is pretty simple,

00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:40.300
currently serving data directly
from Google Cloud Storage

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.970
to users on mobile and desktop.

00:00:42.970 --> 00:00:46.090
Let's take a look at how you can
go about optimizing your window

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:48.280
size, so you can
ensure consistent

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.200
buffering to all
of your clients,

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:52.030
no matter where they are.

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:53.770
To make sure we're
on the right track,

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:56.290
we'll need to profile how
the read performance adjusts

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:58.660
at various window
sizes of download.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:01.420
You can test this by
writing a small script.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:04.239
Create a regional bucket,
create a bunch of files

00:01:04.239 --> 00:01:06.910
at a bunch of
sizes, upload them,

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:11.560
and download each file 100
times via an HTTP fetch request

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:13.540
with caching disabled.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.070
If you run this
script, you'll see

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.870
that there are consistent
results for files

00:01:16.870 --> 00:01:19.090
smaller than 1 megabyte,
which we already

00:01:19.090 --> 00:01:21.380
knew from previous episodes.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.950
And if you run it again,
but tweak it slightly using

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:26.500
a larger file, fetch
through different chunks,

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.630
you'll see that as
chunk size decreases,

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:31.900
the transactional overhead
increases and slows everything

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:32.800
down.

00:01:32.800 --> 00:01:35.170
As we know already,
Google Cloud Storage

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.780
is very powerful in
terms of single stream

00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:40.030
throughput and write
throughput, meaning

00:01:40.030 --> 00:01:42.550
that for both uploads
and downloads,

00:01:42.550 --> 00:01:45.580
Google Cloud Storage is
optimally utilized for requests

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:48.820
that are no smaller
than 1 megabyte in size.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.970
Otherwise, that all too
familiar transactional overhead

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:54.640
is going to bog
things down again.

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.380
With this information,
you can better

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.570
determine what level
of regional redundancy

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:01.180
is needed for serving
your video content.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:04.750
And you can optimize for the 1
meg plus Google Cloud Storage

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.324
target to improve
your buffering speeds.

00:02:07.324 --> 00:02:09.699
There's plenty more to come
about optimizing performance,

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:12.410
but we're going to go ahead
and save that for another time.

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:14.410
If you want to know more,
go ahead and check out

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:15.790
the links in the description.

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.280
And don't forget to
subscribe, give us a like,

00:02:18.280 --> 00:02:20.650
and tell us what you want
to learn about Google Cloud

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:21.550
Storage.

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:24.400
Thanks for joining us for this
quick byte of cloud storage.

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:25.630
See you next time.

